  binding path -- this is what to fix if the clock
  is ever raised:
    from: tme_bd_i/tme_top_0/inst/grp_correlation_core_fu_1826/seg_965_reg_7002_reg[6]/C
    to  : tme_bd_i/tme_top_0/inst/grp_correlation_core_fu_1826/grp_correlation_core_Pipeline_mac_loop_fu_8736/empty_48_fu_1424_reg[23]_psdsp_1/D

  slack at the board's 32.000 ns period: +23.949 ns
  (32 ns is the recorded board period -- CONFIRM it with
   Clocks.fclk0_mhz; the bring-up script now prints it.)

READ THIS BEFORE QUOTING THE WNS ABOVE

The constrained period and the period the board runs at are
different numbers, and the WNS is against the CONSTRAINED one.

THIS IS A CLOCK PROBE, NOT THE SHIPPING IMAGE.  The opened
design is constrained at 8.000 ns, so the PS7 requested
125 MHz and
Vivado constrains the period printed above.  The shipping
image requests 50 MHz, is constrained at 20.000 ns, and runs
on the board at 31.25 MHz / 32.000 ns (contract §8) because
PYNQ applies the PS7's divisor pair to a 1000 MHz PL PLL
rather than the 1600 MHz Vivado assumed.

So this result says the LOGIC closes at the probed period.
It does NOT say the board runs there: reaching it needs the
PS7 FCLK config changed and the achieved rate re-measured
from Clocks.fclk0_mhz on the board.  Until that measurement
exists, quote this as 'closes at <period> in implementation',
never as a board frequency.

The 32 ns slack line above is retained only for continuity
with the shipping image's report.  It is NOT the operating
point this build is about.

Do NOT invert the data delay to get a maximum frequency.  This
build met its constraint with room to spare, at which point
the router stops trying -- the >90% routing share below is
partly an artefact of a loose constraint, not a hard floor.
Re-implement at the target period and read the result.

For comparison, the extractor's standalone image measured
WNS +10.144 ns against the same 20 ns constraint (budget
consumed 9.856 ns, data delay 9.073 ns, 45,918 endpoints,
all constraints met), binding on reset distribution.

